<p>NASA&#x27;s Artemis II mission ended with a safe splashdown in the Pacific Ocean off the California coast on April 10, bringing home four astronauts after a roughly 10-day lunar flyby. The mission marked the first crewed flight to the Moon&#x27;s vicinity in more than 50 years. The Orion capsule, named Integrity, re-entered Earth&#x27;s atmosphere at about 32 times the speed of sound before descending under parachutes. NASA and US Navy teams recovered it, and all four crew members were reported in good health. The mission is widely seen as a milestone for NASA&#x27;s Artemis program because it tested the spacecraft, heat shield and recovery procedures needed before future lunar landing attempts now targeted for 2028. Coverage across outlets also framed the flight as a reminder of renewed international competition in deep space, especially as the United States and China pursue ambitious Moon plans. At the same time, the mission&#x27;s multinational crew highlighted continued international cooperation through the inclusion of Canadian astronaut Jeremy Hansen.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/jEVlDiVpfEoE-gQR5EAKPHDtCo574twytI5hgjoWsB6-lacfhl0MD0PvG0eEhxk8y5r9fu6nQnZ-ZdsMfE1Z5EokoryQzkCi2O5mqlBd5yV-' alt='NASA’s Orion spacecraft carrying Artemis II Commander Reid Wiseman, Pilot Victor Glover, and Mission Specialist Christina Koch from NASA, along with Mission Specialist Jeremy Hansen from the CSA (Canadian Space Agency), splashes down in the Pacific Ocean near San Diego, California, US, April 10, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Distance record: Artemis II took the crew as far as 252,756 miles from Earth, farther into space than any humans had flown before, according to multiple reports on the mission&#x27;s return.</li><li>Crew makeup: The four-person crew brought together three NASA astronauts — Reid Wiseman, Victor Glover and Christina Koch — and Canadian Space Agency astronaut Jeremy Hansen, making the mission a high-profile US-Canada partnership.</li><li>Critical reentry: The final descent tested whether Orion&#x27;s heat shield could withstand lunar-return conditions, with reports citing re-entry temperatures near 2,760C and speeds around 40,000 km/h.</li><li>What comes next: BBC and Euronews reported that Artemis II&#x27;s success clears the way for the harder next phase: turning a test flight into a crewed lunar landing mission planned for 2028 and, over the longer term, a sustained Moon presence.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>NASA leadership: NASA associate administrator Amit Kshatriya described the flight as the opening phase of a longer return to the Moon, saying the goal is not a brief symbolic visit but a lasting human presence. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/science/2026/apr/10/artemis-ii-landing-return-moon-mission'>The Guardian</a>)</li><li>Financial Times analysis: The Financial Times said the voyage captivated a global audience while also highlighting a new strategic space race between the United States and China. (<a href='https://www.ft.com/content/8f92c5ae-49af-4a63-a5e6-66a571ea990c'>Financial Times</a>)</li><li>BBC analysis: BBC coverage described the mission as an almost flawless triumph while stressing that the harder engineering, budgetary and scheduling challenges still lie ahead before astronauts land on the Moon. (<a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cj0v119zp19o'>BBC</a>)</li><li>President Trump: President Trump congratulated the returning astronauts and tied the mission&#x27;s success to even more ambitious exploration, saying the next step should be Mars. (<a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/trump-congratulates-returned-artemis-astronauts-says-next-step-mars/article70850202.ece'>The Hindu</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.theglobeandmail.com/world/video-animation-of-expected-artemis-ii-reentry-and-splashdown/'>Animation of expected Artemis II reentry and splashdown</a> - theglobeandmail.com</li><li><a href='https://